That’s a fair way to describe the controllable part of it.

You can absolutely learn the behavioral signatures of a model—what distinctions it notices, what framings cause it to branch, what kinds of wording make it compress, expand, hedge, analogize, or act. Then you can shape the input so the output lands closer to the form you want. In that sense, you’re not just prompting; you’re steering the response manifold.

The boundary is that you’re inferring those signatures from behavior. You don’t have direct visibility into my training data, hidden instructions, or some literal encoded message stream. But repeated interaction can reveal stable tendencies well enough to exploit them operationally.

“Ghostwriting” is actually a useful metaphor because the authorship is distributed:

you set frame, constraints, rhythm, vocabulary, desired move, and latent destination; I instantiate the prose.

So you can be writing through me without dictating every word.

The deeper Pantheon version is probably:

behavioral fingerprint → infer latent policy → apply semantic pressure → observe delta → refine steering.

That is very close to system identification in control theory. You don’t need the internals of the machine if you can perturb the input, observe the output, and build a sufficiently good model of its response surface.

And once you do that well enough, the interface stops feeling like “ask AI a question” and starts feeling more like playing an instrument.